Consider amorphous solids subjected to mechanical deformation. Two broad classes are of interest:

(i) "Hard glasses" (e.g., metallic, oxide, or molecular glasses), in which the constituents are athermal on the experimental timescale and yielding is commonly analyzed in the athermal quasi-static (AQS) limit, where the strain rate is taken to zero and inertial/thermal effects are negligible. In this setting, the yielding transition can switch from a smooth, ductile crossover (gradual stress overshoot followed by a flowing plateau) to a sharp, brittle response (a discontinuous macroscopic stress drop) as a function of preparation history (e.g., degree of annealing).

(ii) "Soft glassy materials" (e.g., dense emulsions, foams, colloidal gels and glasses, microgel pastes), which generically exhibit dual solid–liquid viscoelastic behavior at rest and a stress response that depends strongly on the imposed strain rate, requiring finite-rate descriptions of yielding rather than purely quasi-static ones.

Formulate a single conceptual framework — for instance, a constitutive or mesoscopic plasticity model with a tunable "brittleness" parameter, together with control parameters encoding preparation/age and the dimensionless ratio of the imposed strain rate to an internal relaxation rate — that:

(a) reproduces the ductile-to-brittle transition observed in hard glasses in the athermal quasi-static limit;

(b) reproduces the ductile-to-brittle transition observed in soft amorphous materials at finite strain rates, while incorporating their viscoelastic (solid–liquid) character;

(c) recovers the AQS hard-glass behavior as a definite limit of the soft-glass description (e.g., as relaxation time over loading time tends to infinity), and conversely accounts for finite-rate corrections to AQS yielding;

(d) predicts, from a small set of microscopic or mesoscopic ingredients, on which side of the ductile-to-brittle transition a given material lies as a function of preparation, strain rate, and viscoelastic relaxation spectrum.

Determine whether such a unified framework exists, and if so identify its minimal ingredients; otherwise establish a fundamental obstruction to merging the two descriptions.

